Theorem CG [MODEL-PROVED in M_CG]:

Let A be any algorithm in M_CG solving at least delta * K targets in expectation (for fixed delta > 0), with total expected bit-work E[W].

1. All-N Universal Floor (including worst-case / special N, adaptive two-stage filtering, and free linear algebra omega = 0):
   (a) Below B_* or under tree-incidence T: If for a positive fraction of solved targets, the extracted dependency D_j uses only prime ideals of norm <= B_* := W^2 (log N)^C (or satisfies tree-incidence T so terminal leaves inherit quota d_j = ceil(3 h_j / 4) >> D log E_Q), then
       E[W] / K >= L_N[1/3, (16/9)^(1/3)] = L_N[1/3, 1.2114]   (allowing ramified k = 1 extraction),
       E[W] / K >= L_N[1/3, (32/9)^(1/3)] = L_N[1/3, 1.5263]   (ring-map k = 2 only; tight for SNFS),
   at rank r = 2 (proved inputs Bean + FT + H_cusp on tiny-quota blocks) and across all ranks r >= 2 under LP_exc^rel (or unconditionally with constant c_A = (4 theta / (3 sqrt(7)))^(2/3) inside the theta-regular KH subclass).
   (b) Arbitrary non-tree descent DAGs / hypergraphs without Assumption T: If D_j uses prime ideals of norm > B_* = W^2 (log N)^C, then under H_cusp (and sublattice LP_exc^rel at r >= 3),
       E[W] / K >= L_N[1/3, (k / 18)^(1/3)]   (0.3816 for k = 1; 0.4807 for k = 2).

2. Generic Single-Target and Amortized Factory Floors (tight for GNFS, MNFS, and Coppersmith's Factory):
   For i.i.d. uniform semiprimes N_1, ..., N_K in [N_0, 2 N_0) (with 1 <= K <= N^(1 - eps) in pure relation mode, or 1 <= K <= N^(1/2 - eps) if batch remainder-tree gcds are permitted alongside M_CG), under GAE-norm:
   - Single target (K = 1):
       E[W] >= L_N[1/3, ((92 + 26 sqrt(13)) / 27)^(1/3)] = L_N[1/3, 1.9019]   at omega = 2 (tight for MNFS),
       E[W] >= L_N[1/3, 1.8595]                                               at omega = 0 (free LA),
     and E[W] >= L_N[1/3, (64/9)^(1/3) = 1.9230] when restricted to V = 1 algebraic side per rational side (tight for GNFS).
   - Batch / Factory (1 <= K <= N^(1/2 - eps)):
       E[W] >= max(L_N[1/3, 1.9019], K * L_N[1/3, 2((2 + sqrt(6))/6)^(2/3)]) = max(L_N[1/3, 1.9019], K * L_N[1/3, 1.6386])   at omega = 2,
       E[W] >= max(L_N[1/3, 1.8595], K * L_N[1/3, 1.6363])                                                                    at omega = 0.

Proof Notes (How C's and G's Mechanisms Combine Without Gaps):

- Closing C's two-stage SNFS/MNFS credit leak: Do NOT leave Stage-0 rational probes uncounted. Apply C's stopping-time supermartingale exp(theta Y - (e^theta - 1) P) to BOTH Stage-0 shared blocks (bounding rational-smooth survivors S_0 from work W_0 >= t_0) and Stage-1 private partner blocks f_v (probed at most S_0 times each, costing sum_v t_v <= Q_j). For V >= 2 partner fields sharing a root mod N_j with g, Axiom NP + Lemma ND prove N_j^2 | disc(g * f_v) for every v AND N_j | Res(f_u, f_v) for all u != v. Hence at most ONE partner field f_{v*} can have height < 1/(2 d) x (or low discriminant); pairing f_{v*} jointly with g enforces the two-sided C2/FT profile A = sqrt(2) (giving 1.5263 even on two-stage SNFS where disc(f_{v*}) is O(1)-independent of N!), while all remaining V - 1 fields satisfy pairwise resultant height >= x / (2 d) (giving >= 1.7114 at omega = 2, >= 1.5263 at omega = 0).
- Recovering 1.9019 and 1.6386 under GAE-norm: By Theorem GAE, for 1 - o(1) of N_j, linear g + degree-d f_v satisfy height floors eta_0, eta_v >= 1/d in F_{2/3}-units (while multiple non-proportional linear sides satisfy N_j | (a_1 b_2 - a_2 b_1), forcing height >= sqrt(N_j) and L_N[1/2]). Applying C's supermartingale to bound Stage-0 yield S_0 <= t_0 L[-1/(3 b_0 d)] and Stage-1 yield R_v <= t_v L[-(1/d + d e)/(3 b_v)] (with t_v <= S_0), enforcing R_v >= B_v^(1-o(1)) on active fields and sum_v R_v >= max(B_0, sum_v B_v)^(1-o(1)) from R, plus W >= W_0 + sum_v t_v + S^omega, yields the exact Coppersmith MNFS program (1.9019 at omega = 2; 1.8595 at omega = 0 where 2e = 2e - r_0 + v binds). Amortizing W_0 across K targets while charging per-target Stage-1 probes t_v and per-target quota max(B_0, B_v) yields 1.6386 (omega = 2) and 1.6363 (omega = 0).
- Closing Step 2 (Assumption-T removal): Because W counts bit operations, sum_{t <= W} log_2 |W(a_t)| <= W, so at most W prime ideals of norm > B_* = W^2 (log N)^C ever appear (forced or unforced) in the entire transcript. By full-cofactor SM2, the expected number of steps t <= W where ANY previously seen prime > B_* (including primes dividing M_t) divides the stripped cofactor C_t, or where any p^2 > B_*^2 divides C_t unforced, is O(W^2 D (log N)^(O(1)) / B_*) = o(1). Thus if L_+(D_j) != empty, at t_last = max_{q in L_+} t_max(q), every odd-valuation prime > B_* of W(a_{t_last}) must divide M_{t_last} and none divides C_{t_last}, making Q = N(M_{t_last}) > B_* with a 100% B_*-smooth cofactor C_{t_last}. Sublattice discriminant scaling |disc((W o M)/Q)| >= N^k Q^((D-1)(D-2)) + Bean + H_cusp forces log C_{t_last} >= sqrt(k x log B_*), which by SM1 in <= W tries forces a >= 1/3 and c >= (k/18)^(1/3).


Z4. THE 3 POINTS A HOSTILE REFEREE WILL ATTACK FIRST (AND WHETHER EACH IS FIXABLE)

1. Point/lattice-probe pricing (SM1 + H_sel + s-stripping) assumes away beyond-Johnson 2D-CRT list decoders (M2) and Prop SI / Bohr smooth constructors:
   Attack: The O~(B) residues of a form mod q^(e_q) (q <= B) are completely N-public and information-theoretically determine every B-smooth point in a box of size L_N[3/4]; charging per-point or per-sublattice probes rather than arbitrary circuits on the residue table rules out capacity-regime algebraic CRT list decoding by definition, not by proof.
   Fixability: NOT fixable unconditionally (proving no poly(B)-time CRT list decoder exists below the Johnson radius is an open superpolynomial arithmetic-circuit lower bound). Completely fixable in theorem framing: state M_CG explicitly as a "priced valuation-probe model" (the smooth-number analogue of Shoup's generic group model), cite Theorem J to cover all oblivious weighted Coppersmith/Howgrave-Graham/Boneh certificates, and isolate sub-Johnson CRT decoding (M2) as the single sharpest algorithmic escape.

2. Farey-cusp exceptions overflowing tiny-quota blocks (h <= O(D log E)) and single-step descent lattices (H_cusp):
   Attack: In C's proof, an adaptive block with B = poly(log N) and D ~ x^(1/3) has O(D log E) ~ x^(2/3) >> floor(h/4), invalidating the FT exception absorption; in G's Assumption-T-free Step 2, t_last is a single step on Lambda_Q where E_Q >= 1, so H_sel does not stop deterministic continued-fraction convergents of M^(-1)(theta_i) from hitting a cusp exception.
   Fixability: FIXABLE for tiny-quota blocks below B_*! When B = (log N)^O(1) (mu = O(log x)), an irreducible degree-D >= 3 Thue-Mahler equation |F(a, b)| = prod_{p <= B} p^(e_p) has at most exp(O(pi(B))) or (by Bombieri-Schmidt / Stewart-Xiao after factoring out S-units, or effective Liouville when D is bounded vs unbounded) tightly controlled primitive solutions; simpler still, restricting blocks to mu >= 2 log x (since mu = O(log x) requires polynomial-time doubly-smooth S-unit generation, which can be treated as its own boundary regime) makes h >= x^2 >> D log E = O(x^(4/3)) whenever log E <= x. For Step 2's single step t_last without Assumption T, H_cusp is NOT removable by elementary area bounds alone (it requires effective Roth/Subspace bounds uniform in D, or retaining Assumption T to get >= d_j distinct leaves).

3. The gap between GAE (coefficient height) and SL_2(R)-invariant sublevel area on sparse adaptive subsets, plus LP_exc^rel and Axiom R:
   Attack: Theorem GAE proves almost all N lack small-height polynomials with N | Res, whereas Bean/FT bound sublevel area via |disc|, which is SL_2(R)-invariant; couldn't an algorithm pick high-height polynomials skewed so a thin sub-box achieves the 1.5263 SNFS norm profile on generic N? Likewise, at rank r >= 3 with O_k^x unit actions (type D), Theorem KH bounds generator heights rather than multi-cone cancelled norms (LP_exc^rel), and Axiom R is unproved for structured polynomial sequences.
   Fixability: PARTIALLY FIXABLE. First, the exponent a >= 1/3 and the constants 1.2114 / 1.5263 at rank 2 do NOT use GAE at all—they hold unconditionally on polynomial shapes for EVERY N and EVERY sparse subset! Second, closing 1.5263 -> 1.9019 on generic N without GAE-norm is fixable in principle if one proves a discriminant/Mahler-measure counting analogue of GAE modulo SL_2(Z) (open for D >= 4). By contrast, LP_exc^rel (r >= 3 unit-cone cancellation) and Axiom R (ruling out sub-B_v short F_2-dependencies on polynomial norms, beyond CGPT/BBM's uniform-integer theorem) are genuine open number-theoretic conjectures that any current model theorem must state as explicit hypotheses.
